# Benchmarks are Release-only (CMakeLists.txt: VC_BUILD_BENCHMARKS "requires
# CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release"), so they are absent from the Debug compilation
# database the root .clangd points at. Without a database of their own, clangd
# has no command for any file here and falls back to a guess with no include
# paths — every bench TU then shows a wall of phantom errors ("undeclared
# identifier 'ankerl'", "no member named 'bench' in namespace 'vc'") that the
# real build does not have.
#
# This pointed at ../cmake-build-bench, which does not exist in this tree. The
# path is now build/bench, which is the `bench` preset's binaryDir, so the one
# command that creates it is the same one you would build with:
#   cmake --preset bench
# Configuring is enough — nothing needs to be compiled, and in particular no
# benchmark should be RUN with --baseline from a source-tree build, which
# overwrites the committed baselines under bench/baselines/.
#
# NOT the `release` preset's build/release: that preset leaves
# VC_BUILD_BENCHMARKS OFF, so pointing here at its directory would both miss
# the bench targets and leave a benchmarks-on cache for the next person who
# runs `cmake --preset release`.
#
# Editor indexing only; the build is unaffected. If the directory is missing,
# clangd degrades to exactly the old behaviour, so this is never worse than
# not having it.
CompileFlags:
  CompilationDatabase: ../build/bench
